00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:04.610 align:start position:0%
over the past weeks we have taken strong  measures to limit the spread of the  corona virus in the European Union  certain national governments have  introduced controls at internal borders  but we have one and a half million  people in the European Union who live in  one country and work in another they  need to cross the border to go to work  many of them have jobs that are  important for us all to get through the  crisis and this is why today we are  publishing practical advice to the  national governments on mobile workers  in the European Union the aim is to  ensure that these workers can reach  their workplace without delays while  taking precautions to keep them safe and  limit the spread of the virus member  states should establish simple and fast  procedures to ensure a smooth passage  for them with proportionate health  screening it is particularly important  that those working in sectors that are  critical to fight the corona virus can  reach their workplace rapidly these are  the people working in the healthcare  sector in childcare elderly care  firefighters and police officers  Transport Workers we need our doctors  nurses and all health workers to be able  to move rapidly from one country to  another for example the vast majority of  people working in hospitals in  Luxembourg live in France or Belgium the  health care system in Luxembourg would  suffer hugely if these workers were  several hours late every day because of  long waiting queues at the borders and  we need food and shops and on our dinner  table so we need those who produce it  and deliver to be able to move - we need  people to plant and harvest our crops we  have to make sure we have enough  seasonal workers in the agriculture  sector they help our farmers and they  want to earn the bread for their loved  ones at home of course under safe  working conditions  many hard-working Europeans help us  combat that virus together we will make  it easier for them to work safely
 

